				"n": 29,
				"id": "ch29",
				"part": "7",
				"title": "Bluetooth and Wireless Survival",
				"html": "<p class=\"lede\">Bluetooth and other wireless protocols are your lifeline in a post-apocalyptic world. From connecting headsets to sharing data between survivors, wireless tech keeps you connected and informed. In a zombie apocalypse, Bluetooth is your best friend.</p>\n<h2>The Basics of Bluetooth Survival</h2>\n<p>Bluetooth is a short-range wireless technology that allows devices to communicate with each other. In a zombie apocalypse, you can use Bluetooth to connect headsets, share data, and even control devices remotely. With a range of up to 100 meters, Bluetooth is perfect for short-range communication.</p>\n<h2>Building a Zombie-Proof Wireless Network</h2>\n<p>You don’t need a fancy router to get started. With a few scavenged parts, you can build your own wireless network. Start with a Bluetooth module, which can be found in old smartphones, laptops, or even cars. Pair it with a microcontroller, and you’ve got the brains of your network. Add a power supply, and you’re ready to roll.</p>\n<h2>The Protocols of Apocalypse Wireless</h2>\n<p>Bluetooth uses a variety of protocols, including Bluetooth Classic and Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE). These standards ensure that your devices can communicate with each other, no matter who built them. In a zombie apocalypse, interoperability is key. You never know what kind of tech you’ll find in a scavenged device, so stick to the standards.</p>\n<h2>Security in the Apocalypse</h2>\n<p>Security is a major concern when the world is falling apart. Bluetooth uses encryption and authentication to protect your data. In a zombie apocalypse, you’ll want to use the strongest encryption available. Look for systems that support AES-128 or higher, and always keep your software up to date. If you’re building your own system, make sure to implement secure key management and regular firmware updates.</p>\n<h2>The Future of Apocalypse Wireless</h2>\n<p>As the world rebuilds, wireless tech will become even more important. With the rise of mesh networks and decentralized communication, you might find yourself building your own wireless network from scratch. Whether you’re using Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, or something entirely new, the future of apocalypse wireless is bright.</p>",
				"plate": "/book/art/ch29.jpg",
				"caption": "Plate 29. Short range, long feelings, one dead battery."
